---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy Policy — Discussed"
  description="Privacy policy for the Discussed browser extension."
>
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1
        class="font-[family-name:var(--font-serif)] text-[clamp(2.5rem,5vw,4rem)] font-normal tracking-[-0.02em] text-[color:var(--text)]"
      >
        Privacy Policy
      </h1>
      <p
        class="mt-4 text-[1rem] leading-[1.8] text-[color:var(--text-muted)]"
        style="max-width:36rem"
      >
        This policy explains what the Discussed extension reads, where data is
        sent, and what is stored in your browser.
      </p>

      <div class="prose prose-sm mt-10 sm:prose-base" style="max-width:42rem">
        <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> October 2, 2026</p>
        <p>
          Discussed finds Hacker News, Reddit, and Lobsters discussions about
          the page you are viewing. It can also summarize those discussions when
          you request it.
        </p>

        <h2>Page URLs</h2>
        <p>
          By default, Discussed searches for related discussions each time a
          page finishes loading. It first normalizes the page URL, removing
          tracking parameters such as <code>utm_*</code> and
          <code>fbclid</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The normalized URL is sent directly from your browser to the Hacker
          News Algolia API and Reddit. Lobsters receives only the page's domain.
          Each enabled source is queried for every page that is searched; there
          is no local pre-check that skips requests.
        </p>

        <h2>Discussion data</h2>
        <p>
          Titles, scores, comment counts, and comments returned by discussion
          services are processed by the extension. Discussion results are cached
          in browser storage for the configured cache duration.
        </p>

        <h2>Page content and AI summaries</h2>
        <p>
          Only when you click <strong>Summarize</strong>, Discussed may read the
          current page's article text and comments on supported sites. This does
          not run automatically when a page loads.
        </p>
        <p>
          Discussion comments and any extracted page text are sent directly from
          your browser to the LLM provider you configured. Your API key is
          stored in browser storage and is sent only to that provider, never to
          Discussed. Generated summaries are cached locally for seven days.
        </p>

        <h2>Third-party services</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.algolia.com/policies/privacy/"
              >HN Algolia API</a
            >
            for Hacker News searches
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.reddit.com/policies/privacy-policy">Reddit</a>
            for Reddit searches
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://lobste.rs/about">Lobsters</a> for Lobsters searches
          </li>
          <li>Your configured LLM provider for user-requested summaries</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Data collection</h2>
        <p>
          Discussed has no backend server or user accounts and does not send
          personal data to Discussed. The extension contains no analytics,
          tracking, or telemetry. The third-party requests described above go
          directly from your browser to those services.
        </p>

        <h2>Local storage and control</h2>
        <p>
          Your API key is kept in local browser storage and is never synced.
          Other settings, including the domain filter, are kept in browser sync
          storage, so if you use your browser's sync feature they are synced
          across your devices through your browser vendor's sync service.
        </p>
        <p>
          Discussion cache duration is configurable. Summaries are retained for
          seven days, and settings and API keys remain until you remove them or
          uninstall the extension. You can disable discussion sources and use
          the domain filter to control where automatic searches run.
        </p>
        <p>
          Removing the extension or clearing its storage removes locally stored
          settings and cached data.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          If you have questions about this policy, contact
          <a href="mailto:hi@discussed.dev">hi@discussed.dev</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
